WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.080
Good morning, Hank!

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:02.080
Wait, who the eff is Hank?

00:00:02.080 --> 00:00:05.620
Hank is a sentient android from the Star Trek:
The Next Generation universe, who, as any

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:10.599
true fan would know, is capable of reproduction
because he had a daughter!

00:00:10.599 --> 00:00:14.700
Aanyway, your video on Monday was one of my
favorite Vlogbrothers videos, ever, but I

00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:17.170
can't believe you got your Star Trek facts
wrong!

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:20.439
Right, so it's Tuesday; I'm gonna upload this
tomorrow after spending all day flying across

00:00:20.439 --> 00:00:24.060
the world with a baby, but right now it's
Tuesday; it's Question Tuesday, the day that

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:25.760
I answer real questions from real Nerdighters!

00:00:25.760 --> 00:00:27.029
"Do you have a vegetable garden?"

00:00:27.029 --> 00:00:30.960
Well, this is my backyard at the moment, so,
no, unless, are cigarettes vegetables? Because,

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:31.960
if so, yes!

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:35.210
"What's up with all the shoes and feet in
the foreign editions of your books?"

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:41.070
Are there a lot of shoes and feet in the foreign
editions of--? Holy crap, there are a lot

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:45.420
of shoes and feet in these foreign editions!
I don't know what's up with that!

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:47.060
"Did something happen with 'Let It Snow?'"

00:00:47.060 --> 00:00:51.090
Yes, Hank, the movie rights to "Let It Snow"
just sold to Paramount, a large movie studio

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:54.540
out there in Hollywood. They also own the
movie rights to "Looking for Alaska," which

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:58.510
they have not yet made, but you never know,
maybe they'll make... one of them. Someday.

00:00:58.510 --> 00:00:59.830
"Can Henry be in today's video?"

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:00.830
Briefly.

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:01.830
"During your stay, have you learned any Dutch?"

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:02.830
Koninginnedag!

00:01:02.830 --> 00:01:05.950
"What are some of the major differences you've
noticed between the U.S. and Europe?"

00:01:05.950 --> 00:01:09.040
Well, the biggest difference is that in the
US, sixty percent of bankruptcies are caused

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:12.780
by medical costs, whereas in the Netherlands,
zero percent of bankruptcies are caused by

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:13.780
medical costs.

00:01:13.780 --> 00:01:14.970
"What's the title of your new book?"

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:20.100
It's still not totally decided, believe it
or not, so I can't tell you yet, but soon...

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.040
"Will you ever restart the Nerdfighter Blurbing
Book Club?"

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:24.860
Now, that's an idea. Hank, it's summertime,
and you know what that means in Nerdfighteria,

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:29.330
it means wearing SPF30 every time you go outside
and reading classic American literature for

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:30.330
fun!

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:32.680
Hank, I hereby announce that the new book
in the Nerdfighter Blurbing Book Club is none

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:36.651
other than The Great Gatsby by F. Scott Fitzgerald.
There's a link to purchase the book in the

00:01:36.651 --> 00:01:40.130
doobly-doo, although, if you don't live in
the United States, it's probably public domain

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:42.170
and you can probably download it for free.
Regardless, it's cheap.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:45.290
It's possible you've already read this book,
and maybe you even hated it, but that's because

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:48.360
you weren't reading it with me! So whether
you're reading it for the first time or reading

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:52.370
it again, that's our book! Let's read it!
The Great Gatsby!

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:54.470
"When are you gonna get Pizza John shirts
back in my size?"

00:01:54.470 --> 00:02:00.320
Now! Bam! I just did it! Dftba.com/pizza!
I am here to solve your problems!

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:02.479
"How do I best confront the urge toward Nihilism?"

00:02:02.479 --> 00:02:06.970
Well, not that kind of problem. Your smaller
problems. You know, pizza shirt related problems.

00:02:06.970 --> 00:02:09.310
"What is the name of the hospital you were
born in?"

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:10.910
...asked the identity thief.

00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:12.531
"Does the Yeti wear DFTBA shirts?"

00:02:12.531 --> 00:02:16.380
Yes, but infuriatingly, she refuses to wear
a Pizza John shirt because she thinks it's

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.950
creepy. I don't know what's so creepy about
it.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:20.100
"Never eat pizza again, or never--"

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:21.970
That one! The one where I can still eat pizza!

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:23.010
"What's Hank's punishment?"

00:02:23.010 --> 00:02:26.280
Hank, I've chosen the punishment that was
most popular in Nerdfighteria, and that is

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:30.420
Humpy Hank All Over Town! I don't know why
Nerdfighters want to see that, but apparently

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:32.860
they do, so that's your punishment. Hump the
town.

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:34.560
"What are your thoughts on Tumblr?"

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.470
My thoughts? I think it's the number one destination
on the internet for images of Paula Dean riding

00:02:39.470 --> 00:02:40.470
me.

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:42.450
"Do you think benign dictatorships are a good
form of government?"

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:45.650
To be honest, I think "benign dictatorship"
is a bit of an oxymoron.

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:47.060
"Who's your biggest inspiration?"

00:02:47.060 --> 00:02:51.240
You. I know that's cheesy, but it's also the
truth. I write books and make videos because

00:02:51.240 --> 00:02:56.520
I am inspired by you to try to tell you stories
that will be interesting and hopefully kind

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:57.520
of helpful?

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:00.840
I've seen my own life changed by books, and
honestly, there's just no greater inspiration

00:03:00.840 --> 00:03:02.819
than the opportunity to do that for other
people.

00:03:02.819 --> 00:03:06.090
"When teachers say 'the author means...' do
you really mean those things?"

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:09.360
Yeah, probably, but in my opinion, teachers
shouldn't say that because 1. teachers aren't

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:13.280
really in the business of reading authors'
minds, and 2. and more importantly, what authors

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:15.720
mean doesn't really matter, I don't think?

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:18.830
What's important is that critical reading
can be a way into thinking quite deeply about

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:22.650
questions that are difficult and complicated,
and not in some, like, boring and abstract

00:03:22.650 --> 00:03:27.459
way, like, "Oh, in Moby Dick, white is a symbol
for nature's ambivalence to man," but instead

00:03:27.459 --> 00:03:31.110
in a concrete and totally interesting way,
like, "Nature's complete indifference to you

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:35.450
as expressed by the color white in Moby Dick
is something that you had better get your

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:38.790
head around, or else you're going to end up
like Captain Ahab!"

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:42.080
So, it's so not much about uncovering secret
mysteries for the sake of uncovering secret

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:46.849
mysteries; it's about using story as a way
into thinking about our actual lives and how

00:03:46.849 --> 00:03:50.240
we're actually living them. And that's how
we're going to try to read The Great Gatsby.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:52.260
Hank, I'm gonna go pack; I will see you on
Friday!

